/** @page oracleora_thread_session_infoguide Per-Thread Session Information

    @tableofcontents

    Oracle reports the \c MODULE, \c ACTION, \c CLIENT_INFO, and \c CLIENT_IDENTIFIER attributes of each session in
    \c V$SESSION (and in the SQL trace, AWR, and ASH data derived from it), so DBAs can see what an application is
    doing in every session.  The driver sets these attributes for the statements executed by a thread from the
    session information set for that thread with
    @ref Qore::Oracle::setThreadSessionInfo() "Oracle::setThreadSessionInfo()":

    @code{.py}
DatasourcePool pool("oracle:user/pass@db%host:1521");

Oracle::setThreadSessionInfo(<OracleSessionInfo>{
    "module": "invoice-export:2.1",
    "action": "batch 1043",
    "client_info": "workflow:INVOICE-EXPORT:2.1",
});
on_exit Oracle::setThreadSessionInfo();

# V$SESSION shows the values above for the session executing this statement
list<hash<auto>> rows = pool.selectRows("select * from invoices where batch_id = %v", 1043);
    @endcode

    The values are set as OCI session attributes on whichever connection executes a statement, commit, or rollback
    for the thread, including pooled connections; OCI sends them to the server with that call, so:
    - no additional statement (such as a \c DBMS_APPLICATION_INFO call) or server round trip is made
    - setting the information does not acquire a connection or start a transaction
    - each statement reports the values of the thread that executes it: a connection used by a thread without
      session information has any values left by another thread cleared, so values never leak between threads that
      share pooled connections
    - an idle connection keeps reporting the values of the last statement, commit, or rollback it executed
    - a connection that is reconnected transparently after a lost connection gets the values again

    Values longer than 64 bytes are truncated on a character boundary.  The information belongs to the thread and is
    freed when the thread terminates; use @ref Qore::Oracle::getThreadSessionInfo() "Oracle::getThreadSessionInfo()"
    to save and restore it around nested code.

    @note The driver only tracks the values that it set itself; setting the same attributes directly with the
    \c DBMS_APPLICATION_INFO or \c DBMS_SESSION packages on a connection used with this API is not supported.
*/
